On the first deployment, there is no old Blue version yet, so version 1.0.1 becomes the initial active version. The real Blue-Green behavior begins when we deploy a second image, such as 1.0.2.

Two Services are the core of Blue-Green deployment.

medical-api-bluegreen-active — receives normal user/production traffic.
medical-api-bluegreen-preview — lets us test the new version before users receive it.

Right now, only version 1.0.1 exists, so both Services may point to the same Pod.

After deploying 1.0.2:

Active Service  → Blue Pod  → 1.0.1
Preview Service → Green Pod → 1.0.2

///////////////////////////////My question how to check which service points to the pod ///////

how to know to which pod points a service

A Service does not point directly to a Pod name. It selects Pods using labels, and Kubernetes creates EndpointSlices containing their IP addresses.

1. See the Service selector
kubectl get service medical-api-bluegreen-active -n medical-bluegreen -o jsonpath="{.spec.selector}"
2. See the exact Pod behind the Active Service
kubectl get endpointslice -n medical-bluegreen -l kubernetes.io/service-name=medical-api-bluegreen-active -o jsonpath="{range .items[*].endpoints[*]}{.targetRef.name}{' -> '}{.addresses[0]}{'\n'}{end}"

Example:

medical-api-bluegreen-7d86c59d67-abc12 -> POD_IP_EXAMPLE_XXX

For Preview:

kubectl get endpointslice -n medical-bluegreen -l kubernetes.io/service-name=medical-api-bluegreen-preview -o jsonpath="{range .items[*].endpoints[*]}{.targetRef.name}{' -> '}{.addresses[0]}{'\n'}{end}"

You can compare those addresses with the Pods:

kubectl get pods -n medical-bluegreen -o wide

The relationship is:

Service selector
→ matching Pod labels
→ EndpointSlice
→ Pod IP

Right now, Active and Preview may show the same Pod because only the initial version exists. After deploying 1.0.2, they should show different Pods until promotion.

/////////////////////////////Question why not ArgoCD used for bluegreen /////





Argo CD alone does not perform Blue-Green switching. It only makes the cluster match what is stored in Git.

The responsibilities are:

Argo CD       → Git synchronization
Helm          → generates Kubernetes manifests
Argo Rollouts → manages Blue/Green Pods and switches Services

We used Helm directly because we are currently testing the unfinished chart safely on the feature branch:

feature/blue-green-rollout

Your existing Argo CD Application still tracks:

targetRevision: main

Therefore, Argo CD cannot see our unmerged Blue-Green changes yet.

The completed flow will be:

Push new image/tag to Git
        ↓
Argo CD detects Git change
        ↓
Argo CD renders the Helm chart
        ↓
Argo Rollouts creates Green Pods
        ↓
Preview Service tests Green
        ↓
Promotion switches Active Service

So we are not replacing Argo CD. We are first proving that the Rollout chart works with Helm. After testing version 1.0.2, we will create a separate local Argo CD Application that tracks:

targetRevision: feature/blue-green-rollout

Then Argo CD will manage the Blue-Green Helm release, and we will stop running helm upgrade manually. This staged approach prevents Argo CD from repeatedly applying an incomplete chart while we are still editing it.


