Yes. That is exactly what autoPromotionEnabled: false is intended to do.

There are two different periods.

Before promotion

With:

autoPromotionEnabled: false

Argo Rollouts should keep both versions running indefinitely:

Active Service  → 1.0.1
Preview Service → 1.0.2
Rollout          → Paused

You can take as long as needed to:

test /health and /ready;
run API tests;
inspect logs;
perform manual QA;
verify database compatibility.

Version 1.0.1 should remain Active until you explicitly promote 1.0.2.

If Green fails testing, abort it instead of promoting:

kubectl argo rollouts abort medical-api-bluegreen -n medical-bluegreen
After promotion

After you promote, Active traffic switches to 1.0.2. This controls how long 1.0.1 remains running afterward:

scaleDownDelaySeconds: 30

Currently it waits only 30 seconds. For a longer post-promotion verification period, change it to five or ten minutes:

strategy:
  blueGreen:
    activeService: {{ include "medical-api.fullname" . }}-active
    previewService: {{ include "medical-api.fullname" . }}-preview
    autoPromotionEnabled: false
    scaleDownDelaySeconds: 600

600 means the old version remains running for ten minutes after the Service switches.

Recommended flow:

Deploy 1.0.2 Preview
→ keep 1.0.1 Active indefinitely
→ run full tests against Preview
→ manually promote
→ keep 1.0.1 alive for another 10 minutes
→ monitor 1.0.2
→ scale down 1.0.1

For your project:

Keep autoPromotionEnabled: false.
Increase scaleDownDelaySeconds from 30 to 600.
Test Green before promotion.
Promote only after tests pass.

Our 1.0.1 disappeared unexpectedly because Helm’s --force-conflicts interfered with the Service selectors. Once Argo CD is configured to ignore the Rollouts-managed selector fields, the normal manual-promotion behavior should work correctly.

